import Layout from '../components/Layout.astro';
import {labels,roles,descriptions} from '../lib/model';
const codes=Object.keys(labels);
---
<Layout title="Methodology" active="Method"><div class="frame"><div class="page-head"><div class="eyebrow"><i></i> RESEARCH NOTES <b>/</b> THIS EDITION</div><h1>Methodology</h1><p>How to read an institutional classification, check a number, and understand the limits of a comparison.</p></div><div class="subbody prose"><section id="personal-trace"><h2>Tracing your own work</h2><p>Enter a workplace and a title to find a profession. Common job titles and aliases lead to a profession card. Where several fields fit, you choose one. The initial trace runs in your browser tab. If you choose Look up sources, Pipeline Map receives the employer and job title for that request. It sends the employer name to participating public indexes and uses the role to check relevance. The search service does not store the submitted fields. You can clear your entries. Shared links and images contain only the public profession record.</p><p>The trace follows the institutions that shaped access for Black professionals. Employer-specific links appear only when a cited source documents a connection for that workplace and profession. A training partnership describes an institutional relationship. Your personal hiring and training history would need its own evidence.</p></section><section id="source-discovery"><h2>Live employer research</h2><p>You can research an employer outside the reviewed directory and a job title outside the profession index. Each result links to the publisher or its source record. A matched source passage describes institutional access related to the work. A research lead needs closer reading before drawing that connection. Live results are separate from reviewed editorial entries and national measurements.</p><p>Public indexes have uneven coverage. An empty result means this search found no matching record. An unavailable index is labeled separately. A wider web search is optional and sends the displayed employer and role terms to the search engine.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/Fuzzyslippers412/pipeline-map-open">Open-source code, data and attribution ↗</a></p></section><section><h2>Origin and gate</h2><p>Origin identifies institutions that helped build access to a profession for Black Americans. Gate identifies institutions proposed as influencing entry today. The seed classifications come from the project brief. Each rationale explains the decision, supporting evidence and its limits. A proposed label is awaiting editorial review.</p><p>A profession can list several institutions. The role lookup uses the first-listed origin and gate. Institutional influence can coexist with a change in representation. These observations do not establish causation.</p></section>
<section><h2>Who is counted</h2><p>Each series follows its publisher’s definition of race, sex, occupation, geography and denominator. The default comparison uses the source’s Black male and Black female categories. Birthplace is unrestricted unless the definition explicitly says otherwise.</p><p>Workforce share uses everyone employed in the matched occupation as its denominator. A training-entry percentage uses the corresponding student cohort. The historical civilian labor force may also include experienced unemployed workers. Choose the relevant measure on each card.</p><p>Percentages use one decimal for display. Charts use straight segments between consecutive comparable observations. The 2003 marker flags a federal race-reporting change. Each source’s actual boundaries apply, and a change of definition or a missing year breaks a line. A single point is one observation.</p><p>Counts come from published tables or weighted survey estimates. ACS estimates use person weights. Where available, the source data include survey sampling uncertainty. The chart does not extrapolate back to 1990.</p></section>
<section><h2>Institutions in the taxonomy</h2><dl>{codes.map(c=><><dt>{labels[c]}</dt><dd>{descriptions[c]}</dd></>)}</dl></section><section id="role-matrix"><h2>The role matrix</h2><p>The brief supplies five specific sentences. Other pairs show the generic match or difference sentence. Open any origin to see every gate. These sentences describe the editorial classification.</p>{codes.map(a=><details class="reading"><summary>{labels[a]}</summary><dl>{codes.map(b=><><dt>{labels[b]}</dt><dd>{roles[a+':'+b]||(a===b?'Origin and gate match':'Origin and gate differ')}</dd></>)}</dl></details>)}</section>
<section id="corrections"><h2>Report an error</h2><p>Use the correction form on GitHub. Identify the profession, source table or editorial classification, describe the issue and link the supporting evidence. The tracker is public.</p><a class="secondary" href="https://github.com/Fuzzyslippers412/pipeline-map-corrections/issues/new?template=correction.yml">Open the correction form ↗</a></section>
<section><h2>Edition notes</h2><p>Research preview · November 2026. Initial institutional assignments, verified source snapshots and a source register. Independent editorial reviews and editor sign-off are pending.</p><p>Pipeline Map is editorial analysis of public data and is independent of the publishers cited. This edition collects no visitor analytics.</p></section></div></div></Layout>
